<odoo noupdate="1">
    <!--
      M9T — record rules. Phase 0 found none at all.

      Two families, deliberately kept apart:

      * COMPANY rules are global (no group). Multi-company isolation is not a
        privilege anybody can be granted past; a manager of company A has no
        business reading company B's cost sheet either.

      * PROJECT rules are opt-in per project. A project with no members named
        behaves exactly as it did before this milestone — everyone in the
        company sees it. Name one member and the project becomes restricted to
        its team. That way enabling access control is a decision somebody makes
        per project, not a migration that locks an entire deployment out on
        upgrade day.

      Managers hold an unrestricted project rule, which ORs with the member
      rule for anybody in both groups.

      That access model is unchanged. What changed is where it is declared.

      Waves 12 to 20 moved the models out and the company rules went with
      them. The project-team and manager rules could not follow: their domains
      read `project_id.construction_member_ids`, this module declared that
      field, and a rule may be declared above the model it guards but never
      below the field it reads. Each receiving module recorded that in its own
      rules file at the time.

      Wave 25 moved the field down to `atmta_construction_core` and the last
      forty-one rules with it, each into the module that owns the model it
      guards. The group reference went from this module's legacy
      `group_construction_user` to the canonical role that group already
      implies, because the canonical role is the one a floor module can name.

      Nothing is declared here any more. The file stays because the paragraphs
      above are the only written account of why construction access works the
      way it does, and deleting it would leave nine modules each holding one
      ninth of the reasoning.
    -->
</odoo>
